  "affectedNativeFlows": [
    {
      "language": "zh",
      "receipt": {
        "path": ".intentfold/tickets/STEMROBIN-181/tmp/d035-n127-science-repair/normalized/zh-flow.json",
        "sha256": "84932c42e2718bf1d4574ec124553adcc45e0d7347e0467b3e8b015aebdc8467"
      },
      "report": {
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "index": 0,
            "blockIndex": 0,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "在国际单位制中，可以利用电磁感应定律来确定磁通量的单位。\n这个单位叫做韦伯。"
          },
          {
            "index": 1,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "感生电动势的单位是伏特，时间的单位是秒。\n因此，根据公式 (12-4)，可以这样定义韦伯：\n**如果穿过一个闭合回路所围面积的磁通量在 1 秒内匀速减小到零，并产生 1 伏特的感生电动势，那么原来的磁通量就是 1 韦伯。"
          },
          {
            "index": 2,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "1 韦伯 = 1 伏特·1 秒。\n**"
          },
          {
            "index": 3,
            "blockIndex": 2,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "公式 (12-1) 给出了确定磁感应强度单位的方法。\n在匀强磁场中，如果矢量 $\\vec{B}$ 垂直于面积为 $S$ 的平面，就有 $\\Phi=BS$。\n因此，如果穿过 1 米$^2$ 面积的磁通量是 1 韦伯，磁感应强度的大小（以特斯拉为单位）就是 1."
          },
          {
            "index": 4,
            "blockIndex": 3,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "前面已经提到，磁感应强度的单位叫做特斯拉。\n1 特斯拉 = 1 韦伯/米$^2$。"
          }
        ],
        "resolvedBreaks": [
          3
        ],
        "boundaries": [
          {
            "index": 3,
            "before": "1 韦伯 = 1 伏特·1 秒。\n**",
            "after": "公式 (12-1) 给出了确定磁感应强度单位的方法。\n在匀强磁场中，如果矢量 $\\vec{B}$ 垂直于面积为 $S$ 的平面，就有 $\\Phi=BS$。\n因此，如果穿过 1 米$^2$ 面积的磁通量是 1 韦伯，磁感应强度的大小（以特斯拉为单位）就是 1."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "receipt": {
        "path": ".intentfold/tickets/STEMROBIN-181/tmp/d035-n127-science-repair/normalized/en-flow.json",
        "sha256": "4e4b125d9968fbf1216b114cbfa8312a74f299d292891fb2e80708318aa94042"
      },
      "report": {
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "index": 0,
            "blockIndex": 0,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "In the International System of Units, the law of electromagnetic induction can be used to establish the unit of magnetic flux.\nThis unit is called the weber."
          },
          {
            "index": 1,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "The unit of induced electromotive force is the volt, and the unit of time is the second.\nTherefore, equation (12-4) gives the following definition of the weber:\n**If the magnetic flux through the area enclosed by a closed loop decreases uniformly to zero in 1 second and produces an induced electromotive force of 1 volt, the original flux is 1 weber."
          },
          {
            "index": 2,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "1 weber = 1 volt·1 second.**"
          },
          {
            "index": 3,
            "blockIndex": 2,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Equation (12-1) gives a way to establish the unit of magnetic flux density."
          },
          {
            "index": 4,
            "blockIndex": 2,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "For a uniform magnetic field, if the vector $\\vec{B}$ is perpendicular to a plane of area $S$, then $\\Phi=BS$."
          },
          {
            "index": 5,
            "blockIndex": 2,
            "paragraphInBlock": 2,
            "text": "Therefore, if the magnetic flux through an area of 1 metre$^2$ is 1 weber, the magnitude of the magnetic flux density, measured in teslas, is 1."
          },
          {
            "index": 6,
            "blockIndex": 3,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "As mentioned earlier, the unit of magnetic flux density is called the tesla.\n1 tesla = 1 weber/metre$^2$."
          }
        ],
        "resolvedBreaks": [
          3
        ],
        "boundaries": [
          {
            "index": 3,
            "before": "1 weber = 1 volt·1 second.**",
            "after": "Equation (12-1) gives a way to establish the unit of magnetic flux density."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
